---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-in-australia`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D in Australia',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D in Australia', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D in Australia: Sunny But Deficient — Cancer Council Guidance and Slip-Slop-Slap"
  description="Why Australia — with world-class UVB availability — has 30% adult deficiency rates. Cancer Council's balance between sun-safety and vitamin D, RCH position statement."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-in-australia", label: "Vitamin D in Australia" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D in Australia</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Australia has one of the world's highest melanoma rates and, in parallel, a
      surprisingly high vitamin D deficiency rate — roughly 30% of adults nationally,
      higher in Victoria and Tasmania in winter. The "slip-slop-slap" sun-safety
      campaigns successfully reduced sun exposure; supplementation and modest
      sensible exposure now fill the vitamin D gap.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Prevalence data</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Australian Health Survey (2011-12) found 23% of adults deficient (25(OH)D
      &lt; 50 nmol/L), rising to 42% in Victoria in winter. Rates markedly higher
      in women (particularly veiled), dark-skinned migrants (60–80%), and
      housebound elderly.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Latitude gradient</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Darwin (12° S) — year-round strong UVB, deficiency mainly from behaviour.
      Sydney (34° S) — year-round with brief winter reduction.
      Melbourne (37° S) — meaningful winter reduction; supplementation useful.
      Hobart (43° S) — Australia's most vitamin D-limited city; winter deficiency
      common; supplementation strongly warranted.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cancer Council balanced position</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>UV Index ≥ 3</strong> (most of Australia, most of the year): a few minutes of exposure to face + arms most days provides adequate vitamin D. Sun protection required for extended exposure.</li>
      <li><strong>UV Index &lt; 3</strong> (winter in southern Australia, early morning/late afternoon most times): about 2–3 hours per week of outdoor exposure to face, arms, hands may be needed.</li>
      <li><strong>Supplementation</strong> recommended for people at risk of deficiency or when sun exposure is difficult.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">NHMRC recommended dietary intakes</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Ages 1–50:</strong> 5 µg/day (200 IU) — lower than US/Canada RDAs; reflects earlier consensus.</li>
      <li><strong>Age 51–70:</strong> 10 µg/day (400 IU).</li>
      <li><strong>Age 71+:</strong> 15 µg/day (600 IU).</li>
      <li>Note: many Australian clinicians now use higher targets, closer to US Endocrine Society levels.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Testing 25(OH)D in Australia</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Medicare covers 25(OH)D testing under specific clinical indications
      (osteoporosis, malabsorption, hyperparathyroidism, chronic kidney disease,
      dark skin, veiled women, chronic drug use). Restrictions tightened
      2014–2020 to reduce inappropriate testing. Private testing is available for
      $50–100 out of pocket.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Australian dosing practice</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Standard OTC 1,000 IU cholecalciferol widely available; often called "Ostelin" branded product.</li>
      <li>Prescription 25,000 or 50,000 IU capsules for correction of documented deficiency.</li>
      <li>Winter maintenance 1,000–2,000 IU/day for southern-Australia adults.</li>
      <li>Year-round supplementation for dark-skinned, veiled, or housebound Australians.</li>
      <li>Combine with sensible sun (a few minutes outdoor time when UV is ≥ 3), never with sunburn.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Australia leads the world in melanoma
      screening — an annual skin check is part of good adult health here, and
      matters more than any vitamin decision.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-in-australia" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
